"""P3: the condition rule asks its own question instead of guessing from spelling.

`every row states its fare in a sentence, one disclosure away` asserted that a
restricted row's condition does not open with one to four capitals. That was a
proxy for "is not merely the code again", and on the real feed it rejects 624 of
the 841 descriptions in the file -- `OFF-PEAK`, `NTH ADVANCE`, `NOT VALID BEFORE
0915 MON-FRI`, `BAN ALL DAY`. Those are the conditions a reader needs, they are
the feed's own wording, and paraphrasing them is a licencee's risk.

Counted over the whole real `.RST`: zero descriptions are empty and zero equal
their own restriction code, so the page has never printed a bare code here. The
condition in the panel is the feed's wording, short and in capitals because the
feed writes it that way.

So the rule asks what it meant to. A condition must be present and must not be
its code restated: not equal to it, not built only from its letters, and not the
code with a digit on the end. `OFF-PEAK` beside `B3` passes, because it says
something the code does not. `B3`, `b3`, `BB333` and `B31` beside `B3` all fail.

The rule also gets the code to compare against -- `record[1]` is the
restriction, which the read was already carrying -- and a check of its own that
names the defect in words, so "no row prints its restriction code as though it
were the condition" is a line a reader can hold the page to.

The examples that separate the two rules are in the suite, not only here. A rule
that has been wrong in the generous direction will be wrong in the other one
next, and the cases that catch that belong next to the rule rather than in the
commit that happened to write it.

Verified on the real Grindleford page: 194 of 194, up from 191. The synthetic
sweep is 671 of 671. And a deliberately broken copy of the Grantham page -- one
line changed so the panel prints the code where the condition belongs -- fails
both condition checks, which is the proof that stopping the complaint was not
the same as stopping the test.

Generated by _cache/make_p3.py, which reads every anchor out of git and off
disk, so an anchor cannot drift from the text it is meant to match.
"""

